# Body Blueprints — Plan

## Core idea
Heading blueprints gave every note structured **metadata** (properties/drawer).
Body blueprints give every note structured **content** — a defined shape for what goes in the body itself, not just the properties.

Same pattern, one level down: a concept at the top, its parts nested under it — now applied to writing, not just state-tracking.

## What a body blueprint is
A file (like a heading blueprint file) that defines:
- A note-type name (e.g. "Definition Note", "Experiment Note", "Person Note")
- A fixed set of body sections that belong to that type
- Each section can be: required or optional, plain text, or itself a nested sub-structure

Example shape (conceptual, not final):
```
* DEFINITION_NOTE
** Definition (required)
** Examples (optional)
** Counter-examples (optional)
** Related concepts (optional, links only)
```

## The key new capability: nesting
A heading property can already "go live" and become its own note (via the existing property-to-unfolding-note flow).
Body blueprints need the same thing at the section level:
- A body section can be plain text (leaf), OR
- A body section can itself point to a sub-blueprint (branch) — meaning that section, once filled in, becomes its own structured sub-note or sub-template, not just free text

This is what makes it "manifolding" instead of just "template": concepts can contain concepts, at any depth, the same mechanism reused recursively.

## The wormhole connection
Instead of copy-pasting a static template into a note's body, a body-blueprint section should be **live-linked** to its defining rule/structure note (using the existing wormhole/transclusion mechanism).
Benefit: if you improve a note-type's structure later, existing notes referencing that structure can stay in sync, instead of every past note being frozen with an old template.

## Why this matters (the actual goal)
Every note, of any type, connected to anything or nothing, should be understandable completely on its own — no missing context, no assumed background. Body blueprints are what guarantee a note's *shape* enforces that completeness, the same way heading blueprints guarantee a note's *state* is always trackable.

This matches an established idea from note-taking theory (Zettelkasten "permanent notes"): a note must be self-contained and understandable without needing to trace back to its sources. Body blueprints are the mechanism that enforces this by construction, not by discipline/memory.

